{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1594776539/9/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b7955b3c\" lines=\"386-403\">\n<description>\nReplace gray plus-shaped damage masks with the hidden colors of the rectilinear mosaic.\n\nInput structure:\n- Color 8 appears only as isolated 3x3 plus masks.\n- The non-mask pixels are visible parts of a clean grid made from horizontal\n  and vertical color regions.\n\nRule:\n- Treat every color-8 pixel as unknown damage and leave all other pixels fixed.\n- First copy unknown pixels from exact matching parallel rows or columns.\n- Then fill any remaining mask cells from opposite-side continuations and the\n  better-supported local straight edge.\n\nKey insight:\n- The gray pluses are occluders, not objects; the underlying colors are\n  determined by repeated line templates and nearby axis-aligned boundaries.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b5bb5719\" lines=\"17-32\">\n<description>\nFill the background under a top-row red/grey span with a shrinking diagonal pattern that alternates red and grey.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has an orange background.\n- All foreground pixels are red or grey and lie on the top row.\n- The first and last top-row foreground pixels define an odd-width span.\n\nRule:\n- Copy the input grid.\n- For row r below the top, fill columns span_left + r through span_right - r.\n- Each filled cell takes the top-row color r columns up-left, flipped red<->grey once per row step.\n\nKey insight:\n- The output is the top-row span propagated down-right along diagonals, while the allowable region shrinks inward from both ends each row.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"d8e07eb2\" lines=\"29-47\">\n<description>\nLook up query glyphs in a reference table and mark whether they form a line.\n\nInput structure:\n- Teal background with two full-width pink separator rows.\n- Above the first separator is a query row of 5x5 glyph cells.\n- Between separators is a reference table of the same 5x5 cells; a glyph is\n  identified by both its color and its 3x3 shape.\n\nRule:\n- Green-fill the background inside every table cell whose glyph exactly\n  appears in the query row.\n- If the matched table cells form one complete row or one complete column,\n  also green-fill the query band and the bottom verdict band.\n- Otherwise leave the query band unchanged and fill the bottom verdict band red.\n\nKey insight: The bottom color is not a count; it is the yes/no answer to\nwhether the queried glyphs align as a full row or column in the table.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2b9ef948\" lines=\"51-65\">\n<description>\nMove the yellow 3x3 frame by the displacement encoded by the gray path, then draw its diagonal yellow corner rays.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is black.\n- One 3x3 yellow frame has a non-yellow control-color center.\n- A second control-color pixel is connected through gray path pixels to a lone yellow endpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Use the vector from the second control-color pixel to the lone yellow endpoint as the frame's translation.\n- Fill the whole output with the control color.\n- Draw the translated yellow frame and extend yellow diagonal rays outward from its four corners to the grid edge.\n\nKey insight: The gray path's turns are irrelevant; its two colored endpoints encode the net displacement to apply to the frame.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9720b24f\" lines=\"34-46\">\n<description>\nDelete each foreign-color object whose bounding box is contained by a larger object of another color.\n\nInput structure\n- The black pixels are the empty canvas, and every other color forms one or more 8-connected monochromatic objects.\n- Some larger foreground objects act as loose U/C-like containers around smaller objects of different colors.\nRule\n- Find all 8-connected monochromatic non-black objects.\n- Recolor an object to black exactly when its bounding box is contained in the bounding box of a larger object of another color.\n- Keep all other pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\n- The removable objects are identified by containment in another color's rectangular extent, not by their own color or shape.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b3084d4\" lines=\"253-266\">\n<description>\nAssemble colored rectangular sprites into a single tiled rectangle, anchored by a marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several solid-colored rectangular sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One sprite has a unique single-pixel marker (different color) at one corner — this is the anchor.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the anchor sprite (contains the marker pixel) and the remaining pieces.\n- Rotate the anchor so the marker pixel lands at position (0,0) of the output.\n- Pack all remaining pieces (allowing 90° rotations) to tile a complete rectangle with no gaps.\n\nKey insight: The lone marker pixel picks the anchor and fixes the output orientation — it must sit at the top-left corner.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"db0c5428\" lines=\"32-51\">\n<description>\nExpand a 3x3 block motif into a 5x5 block motif around its missing center.\n\nInput structure:\n- Teal background surrounds one 9x9 foreground motif.\n- The motif is a 3x3 array of 3x3 blocks; the middle block is empty teal.\n- Colored pixels immediately around the empty middle block encode the border\n  of the missing center block.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the original 9x9 motif in place.\n- Copy each of the eight surrounding 3x3 blocks to the opposite outer slot\n  of the implied 5x5 block grid, without rotating it.\n- Fill the center block from the eight pixels around the hole; its center is\n  the motif's majority foreground color.\n\nKey insight: The visible 9x9 motif is the middle of a larger periodic\nblock-level pattern, and the empty center is reconstructed from its immediate\ninner boundary.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"17829a00\" lines=\"21-36\">\n<description>\nMove each colored object vertically until it touches the matching top or bottom border bar.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The top row is a full-width bar in one foreground color and the bottom row is a full-width bar in another.\n- All other foreground pixels are 8-connected monochrome objects whose color matches one of those bars.\n\nRule:\n- Top-bar-colored objects move upward so their bbox top is row 1.\n- Bottom-bar-colored objects move downward so their bbox bottom is the row above the bottom bar.\n- A downward-moving one-column segment whose landing overlaps its source becomes the continuous vertical segment swept between them.\n\nKey insight:\nThe horizontal bars are destination anchors: color determines which border an object slides toward, while columns and object shape are otherwise preserved.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"342ae2ed\" lines=\"34-49\">\n<description>\nDraw the missing diagonal connector segment between each pair of same-color rectangular objects.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground color appears as exactly two separated solid rectangular 4-connected objects.\n- The two rectangles of each color are diagonally offset with facing corners on a 45-degree line.\n\nRule:\n- Keep all existing rectangles unchanged.\n- For each foreground color, connect the lower facing corner of the upper rectangle to the upper facing corner of the lower rectangle.\n- Paint only the intervening background pixels on that diagonal, excluding the two endpoint pixels already in the rectangles.\n\nKey insight:\n- Same-color rectangles define the endpoints; the output completes the open diagonal between their nearest facing corners.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6165ea8f\" lines=\"74-90\">\n<description>\nBuild a color-labeled shape-equivalence table from the objects listed on the right edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background contains one main 4-connected object for each foreground color.\n- The rightmost column has one singleton pixel of each foreground color, listed top-to-bottom.\n- Those singleton pixels define the row and column order of the output table.\n\nRule:\n- Normalize each main object to its tight mask and compare masks up to rotation or reflection.\n- Draw 2x2 color labels across the top and down the left, separated by one background row/column.\n- For each off-diagonal table cell, paint a 2x2 red block when the two labeled objects have the same shape under rotation/reflection, otherwise paint a 2x2 grey block.\n- Leave diagonal cells and all separators as background.\n\nKey insight:\n- Object positions and original orientations are distractors; the relation is dihedral shape equivalence, while the right-edge legend fixes the table order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dd6b8c4b\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nMove the closest reachable maroon pixels into the 3×3 block around the unique red center.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = orange floor, magenta walls, scattered maroon (9) pixels.\n- A unique red (2) pixel marks the center of a 3×3 destination block.\n\nRule:\n- Maroon pixels connected to the destination through floor/maroon cells (not blocked by walls) are candidates.\n- Rank candidates by distance to the destination; the closest ones fill the block in row-major order.\n- Moved maroons are erased (replaced with floor color).\n\nKey insight: Wall barriers restrict connectivity — only maroon pixels reachable via flood-fill through the floor qualify, so proximity alone isn't enough.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6350f1f4\" lines=\"62-81\">\n<description>\nDecode a block-grid image by swapping each sprite-coded cell with a solid cell and each blank cell with the sprite.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is partitioned by black separator rows and columns into equal-size\nsquare blocks. Each block is either a copy of one two-color sprite or a\nuniform blank block of the sprite's minority color; grey pixels are erased\ncells that act as wildcards.\n\nRule\nInfer the repeated sprite from fully visible mixed-color blocks. Replace a\nblock matching that sprite, ignoring grey erasures, with a solid block of\nthe sprite's majority color. Replace a uniform blank block with the sprite.\nSeparator rows and columns, including grey erasures on them, become black.\n\nKey insight\nThe small sprite is a block-level code for one macro-cell state, and the\nuniform minority-color block is the other state; the output renders the\nopposite code in each macro-cell while cleaning erased pixels.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
